; Leg BA -- PORTING.md SS16af leg AG repeated, FOR THE HOST CLOCK.
;
; No code change and no new question about the port.  The one thing this leg
; adds to AG is the file it is redirected into: the host's "statistics" has
; millisecond resolution and the Victor's clock advances in 50 cs steps, and
; AG was run without the redirect.  SS16af and SS16ae both lost this figure.
;
; WHAT IT IS FOR.  SS16af's cleanest result is that CRC-16 now costs ONE
; CLOCK QUANTUM over a 6-bit checksum -- 100 +/- 50 cs, i.e. 13 to 40 us per
; wire byte.  Against the 60-90 8088 cycles that separate the two block
; checks, that band admits an effective clock anywhere from 1.5 to 7 MHz, so
; the model cannot be calibrated from it.  BA and BB together replace the
; band with a number, and every per-item estimate in this port is quoted
; against that number afterwards.
;
; Targets to reproduce, from SS16af leg AG (Victor clock):
;     rxfull 0, rxpeak 2,581, rxbytes 37,568, 18 packets, 0 resends,
;     elapsed 2,800 cs.  Host figure predicted near 26.3 s and ~1,245 cps.
;
; Read in this order: cmp RCVBA.DAT rcvba.dat (byte-exact or void), then
; rxfull, then rxbytes, then the host elapsed time -- which is the point.
;
; set line and the adapter come from ~/.kermrc.  CHECK IT MATCHES the
; adapter actually plugged in: SS16v used BG022B8M and SS16af ABBFKXM1.

set speed 38400
set receive timeout 20
set parity none
set prefixing cautious
set block 3
log packets s16ahBA.pkt
send rcvba.dat
statistics
